EV_ABS must be tested independently of EV_REL, not as its else.

WITHOUT THIS THE SYSTEM HAS NO POINTER. labwc ran, the desktop drew, the
keyboard worked -- and libinput enumerated exactly two devices:

    [backend/libinput/events.c:69] Adding QEMU Virtio Keyboard [1575:1]
    [backend/libinput/events.c:69] Adding gpio-keys [1:1]
    [backend/libinput/backend.c:125] libinput successfully initialized

No third line, and NO REJECTION MESSAGE EITHER -- which is what makes this hard
to find. libinput does not decline the tablet; it never considers it.

THE DEVICE IS FINE AND SO IS QEMU. `cat /dev/input/event1 | od -c` shows the
kernel emitting events as the pointer moves, and the QEMU monitor reports

    * Mouse #2: QEMU Virtio Tablet (absolute)

so the host is offering an active absolute pointer and the guest kernel is
delivering it. Everything between them was working.

WHAT set_properties_from_evdev DOES, AND WHY THE TABLET FALLS THROUGH.
udev_device.c:457 is

    if (test_bit(ev_bits, EV_REL)) {
        if (REL_X && REL_Y && BTN_MOUSE) -> ID_INPUT_MOUSE
    }
    else if (test_bit(ev_bits, EV_ABS)) {
        ... ID_INPUT_TABLET / TOUCHSCREEN / TOUCHPAD / MOUSE ...
    }

The QEMU virtio-tablet reports, from /proc/bus/input/devices:

    B: EV=f        SYN | KEY | REL | ABS
    B: REL=100     bit 8 == REL_WHEEL ONLY.  No REL_X, no REL_Y.
    B: ABS=3       ABS_X | ABS_Y
    B: KEY=30400 1f0000 ...   includes BTN_LEFT (0x110 == BTN_MOUSE)

So EV_REL is set -- the device has a scroll wheel -- and the first branch is
entered. Inside it the REL_X/REL_Y test fails, because a wheel is not an axis
pair. Nothing is assigned. AND BECAUSE THE SECOND BRANCH IS AN `else if`, THE
ABS BLOCK IS NEVER REACHED, so the ABS_X/ABS_Y + BTN_MOUSE case that describes
this device exactly is never evaluated.

The device ends with ID_INPUT=1 and NO TYPE PROPERTY. libinput's contract is
explicit about what happens then -- from its own documentation: "libinput
requires the ID_INPUT property to be set on a device, otherwise the device will
be ignored. In addition, one of ID_INPUT_KEYBOARD, ID_INPUT_KEY,
ID_INPUT_MOUSE, ID_INPUT_TOUCHPAD, ID_INPUT_TOUCHSCREEN, ID_INPUT_TABLET,
ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK, ID_INPUT_ACCELEROMETER must be set on the device to
determine the device type." One property without the other means silently
ignored.

REL AND ABS ARE NOT MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL BUG. A device may
have both, and many do: any absolute pointer with a scroll wheel, which is
every QEMU tablet and a good deal of real hardware. Upstream udev's input_id
builtin tests the capability bits INDEPENDENTLY rather than as an either/or,
which is why this system is the one that trips over it -- a machine running the
real udev daemon would never see the failure.

WHAT CHANGES AND WHAT DOES NOT. Only the ABS block becomes reachable when
EV_REL is also present; the REL block is untouched, and every assignment inside
the ABS block keeps its existing guards and order. Checked against the device
classes this system actually has:

    QEMU Virtio Keyboard   KEYBOARD  -> KEYBOARD    unchanged
    gpio-keys              KEY       -> KEY         unchanged
    ordinary USB mouse     MOUSE     -> MOUSE       unchanged
    touchpad               TOUCHPAD  -> TOUCHPAD    unchanged
    QEMU Virtio Tablet     (none)    -> MOUSE       FIXED

A device that legitimately matched both branches would now get two properties
rather than one. That is what real udev does, and libinput accepts it: the
properties describe capabilities, not an exclusive category.

WHY NOT JUST USE virtio-mouse-pci INSTEAD. A pure relative mouse takes the
first branch correctly and works -- but a relative pointer requires the
HYPERVISOR TO GRAB AND WARP THE HOST CURSOR to synthesise deltas, which it does
by yanking the user's real pointer back to the centre of the window. The tablet
exists precisely to avoid that. Choosing the broken-but-classified device over
the correct one would trade a fixable bug for a permanent annoyance.

